## Phase 1: Pre-Upgrade & Safeguards

Before running any update on production, ensure the infrastructure is entirely locked down and backed up.

1. #### Enable the Maintenace warning
   Edit the nginx conf to display a banner to current users. This will display when they refresh any page and should be implimented 15 mins befor the next step that will block all users :-
   ```
   sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/suitecrm.conf
   ```
   Look for the section "--- Maintenance Warning and uncomment the last 2 lines.
   ```
    # --- Maintenace Warning ---
    # This inject a global warning banner at the top of the body,
    # To activate Maintenace warning, uncomment the next 2 lines.
    # sub_filter '<body>' '<body><div style="background-color: #e74c3c; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 12px; font-weight: bold; font-size: 16px; position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; z-index: 99999; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px>
    # sub_filter_once on;
   ```
   Test and restart nginx
   ```
   sudo nginx -t

   sudo systemctl reload nginx
   ```



1. #### Enable Maintenance Mode
   Edit the nginx conf prevent users from accessing the system, execept for a single permitted IP address.
   ```
   sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/suitecrm.conf
   ```
   Look for the section "--- Maintenance Mode and uncomment the last 2 lines.
   ```
    # --- MAINTENANCE MODE CONFIGURATION ---
    # To activate maintenace mode uncomment the following 2 lines 
    #set $maintenance on;
    set $permited_addr "90.219.190.144";
   ```
   n.b. leave "set $permited_addr uncommented or nging will not reload
   
   Test and restart nginx
   ```
   sudo nginx -t
   
   sudo systemctl reload nginx
   ```

1. #### Backup database.
   As the database sits on a common sql cloud instance with wordpress and uat, we need to export the individual database not backup the entire sqlinstance.

   - Log into Google Cloud Console and navigate to SQL.
   - Click on your shared Cloud SQL instance name "vinttro-sql".
   - Click the Export button at the top of the Overview page.
   - Configure the Export settings:
     ```
     File format: Select SQL.

     Data to export: Toggle this to Specific databases (do NOT leave it on "All databases").

     Database dropdown: Select only your production SuiteCRM database (e.g., prod_suitecrm).

     Destination: Click Browse and select a Google Cloud Storage (GCS) bucket to store the file. Append a clear filename to the bucket path, like:

     gs://vinttro-temp-storage/prod_suitecrm_2026-06-02 (16:37:25).sql
   

     Click Export. Wait for the Cloud SQL operations log to show that the export completed successfully.

1. #### Backup file system.
   Although we have a VM snapshot, this is a last resort. Having a backup of the suitecrm file sructure is sensible as this could be used to compare or just restore individual files.

   ```
   cd /tmp
   tar -czf suitecrm_files_backup_$(date +%F).tar.gz /var/www/suitecrm/
   ```

1. #### Verify PHP Compatibility  
   Ensure your production PHP version matches the requirements for the target SuiteCRM version (SuiteCRM 8.7+ generally requires PHP 8.1 or 8.2).
   ```
   php -v

   PHP 8.2.31 (cli) (built: May  8 2026 07:20:38) (NTS)
   Copyright (c) The PHP Group
   Zend Engine v4.2.31, Copyright (c) Zend Technologies
       with Zend OPcache v8.2.31, Copyright (c), by Zend Technologies
   ```

1. #### Increase PHP Limits:
   Temporarily ensure your `php.ini` values are generous enough to handle large package uploads:
    - `memory_limit = 512M` (or higher)
    - `max_execution_time = 300`
    - `upload_max_filesize = 100M`
    - `post_max_size = 100M`



